Sure, based on the comments, I will add the corresponding debug.assert statements in C#:

```cs
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList.Remove(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

            // The old index of 'x' should be greater or equal to -1 because 'x' is in the array list before remove operation
            // The new index of 'x' should be equal to -1 because 'x' is removed from the array list
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1, "The old index is not correct.");
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1, "The value 'x' is not correctly removed from the list.");
	    // If 'x' is not in the list, LastIndexOf and IndexOf should return -1.
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1, "The LastIndexOf is not correct.");
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == false, "The 'x' is still in the list.");

        }
    }
}
```
In the code above, I added Debug.Assert statements to validate the assumptions made in the comments. The first two assertions ensure that the old index of 'x' is either -1 (means 'x' is not in the list) or a positive number (means 'x' is in the list but we don't know at which position), and the new index of 'x' is -1 (since 'x' should be removed from the list). The next two assertions ensure that if 'x' is not in the list, LastIndexOf and IndexOf should return -1.
--------------------Using the provided csharp code, the assertions are as follows:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1); // index of x in old array is geq minus 1
```

Debug.Assert is a method provided by the .NET framework for development purposes. It is basically an if statement that executes only when the condition is false. It is meant to be used during debugging. Its main advantage is that it doesn't affect the performance of your program, unlike a conditional breakpoint in most debuggers. This assertion checks that the index of the removed element 'x' is not less than -1. This means that if 'x' is not in the array, its index remains unchanged. If 'x' was located at an index greater or equal to 0, it would not be found in the array after the 'Remove' operation.
--------------------The corresponding assert statements would be:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
```

This asserts that the index of the value x in the old array is greater than or equal to -1. In a context where `IndexOf` would return -1 if the value was not found in the array, this ensures that the value was present in the array before it was removed.
--------------------Here is the C# code using "Debug.Assert" for the comments that start with "@@@":

```cs
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList.Remove(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // @@@ index of x in old array is geq minus 1
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);

        }
    }
}
```

In this code, the assertion `Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);` checks whether the index of `x` in the old array list is greater or equal to -1. This means that the `x` is either in the list or not in the list (if `x` is not in the list, `IndexOf(x)` return -1).
